      },
      "rev_n_1_7dd3f0_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_1_7dd3f0_0",
        "node_id": "n_1_7dd3f0",
        "thought_text": "Analyze the problem by grouping students by their original schools and identifying the maximum power in each group. For each 'Chosen One', check if their power is less than the maximum power of their current school. If so, they need a new school. The minimal number of new schools is simply the count of Chosen Ones who are not currently the strongest in their respective schools.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "root",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794589158.9464636
      },
      "rev_n_2_fcdb35_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_2_fcdb35_0",
        "node_id": "n_2_fcdb35",
        "thought_text": "Simulate the selection process: iterate through all students, find the max power for each existing school, then count how many Chosen Ones fail this condition. This direct simulation confirms the logic without needing complex graph algorithms or dynamic programming, as the constraints are small (n <= 100).",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "root",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794589158.9464786
      },
      "rev_n_3_89f8dd_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_3_89f8dd_0",
        "node_id": "n_3_89f8dd",
        "thought_text": "Consider edge cases where a Chosen One might be the strongest but still needs a move due to some hidden constraint, or where moving one student affects others. Re-evaluate the problem statement: moving a student to a new school only helps them; it doesn't negatively impact other schools because the new school is unique to them. Thus, the greedy approach of counting non-strongest Chosen Ones remains optimal.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "root",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794589158.9464867
      },
      "rev_n_4_04a7f9_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_4_04a7f9_0",
        "node_id": "n_4_04a7f9",
        "thought_text": "Group students by their original schools to find the maximum power in each school. For every Chosen One, check if their power is strictly less than the maximum power of their current school. If it is, they must be moved to a new school. The answer is the count of such students.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-4078704552797119562",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794589186.2515168
      },
      "rev_n_5_ffb735_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_5_ffb735_0",
        "node_id": "n_5_ffb735",
        "thought_text": "Simulate the selection process by sorting all students within each school by power. Iterate through the list of Chosen Ones and verify if each appears at the top of their school's sorted list. Count how many do not appear at the top, as these require new schools.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-4078704552797119562",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794589186.2515328
      },
      "rev_n_6_edd87e_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_6_edd87e_0",
        "node_id": "n_6_edd87e",
        "thought_text": "Construct a frequency map of the maximum power for each school ID. Then, iterate through the Chosen Ones' IDs, look up their school, compare their power against the stored maximum, and increment a counter if they are not the strongest. This avoids re-sorting and runs in linear time relative to n.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-4078704552797119562",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794589186.2515426
      },
      "rev_n_7_19db1c_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_7_19db1c_0",
        "node_id": "n_7_19db1c",
        "thought_text": "Iterate through each of the $m$ original schools, identify the student with the maximum power in that school, and count how many 'Chosen Ones' are not the maximum in their respective schools. This count is the answer.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-8330862019085185594",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794589199.130922
      },
      "rev_n_8_7fabea_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_8_7fabea_0",
        "node_id": "n_8_7fabea",
        "thought_text": "Sort all students by power in descending order. Simulate the selection process: for each school, the first student encountered who belongs to that school is the winner. Check if any Chosen One is skipped by this simulation; the number of skipped Chosen Ones is the result.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-8330862019085185594",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794589199.130938
      },
      "rev_n_9_078557_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_9_078557_0",
        "node_id": "n_9_078557",
        "thought_text": "Construct a frequency map of the maximum power for each school. For every Chosen One, compare their power to the stored maximum of their school. If their power is less, increment the counter. This avoids sorting and runs in linear time relative to $n$.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-8330862019085185594",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794589199.1309466
      },
      "rev_n_10_213b3b_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_10_213b3b_0",
        "node_id": "n_10_213b3b",
        "thought_text": "Iterate through each school to find the current strongest student. For every Chosen One who is not the strongest in their school, increment a counter. The answer is simply this count, as each such student requires exactly one new unique school.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-8796644944954208518",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794589209.4856555
      },
      "rev_n_11_6c1ed5_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_11_6c1ed5_0",
        "node_id": "n_11_6c1ed5",
        "thought_text": "Sort all students by power in descending order. Process them one by one; if a student is a Chosen One and their school already has a stronger student assigned to it, they must be moved to a new school. Count these moves.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-8796644944954208518",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794589209.4856846
      },
      "rev_n_12_fa352c_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_12_fa352c_0",
        "node_id": "n_12_fa352c",
        "thought_text": "Use a greedy simulation: maintain a set of 'winners' for each original school. For each Chosen One, check if they are in the winner set. If not, move them to a new virtual school and increment the cost. This avoids complex data structures given n <= 100.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-8796644944954208518",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794589209.485693
      },
      "rev_n_8_7fabea_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_8_7fabea_1",
        "node_id": "n_8_7fabea",
        "thought_text": "Thought-2 (corrected): The logic to count non-strongest Chosen Ones is correct, but the implementation contains a critical indexing error where `s[s[cid]]` incorrectly looks up the school of the student whose ID equals the current student's school ID, instead of directly accessing the school of the current student. The condition should simply compare the chosen student's power against the maximum power of their actual school using `s[cid]`.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-8330862019085185594",
        "replaces_revision_id": "rev_n_8_7fabea_0",
